Let me look at the `StringToNumber` for `float` and `double`:

```cpp
template<> inline bool StringToNumber(const char *s, float *val) {
  return StringToFloatImpl(val, s);
}

template<> inline bool StringToNumber(const char *s, double *val) {
  return StringToFloatImpl(val, s);
}
```

And `StringToFloatImpl`:
```cpp
template<typename T>
inline bool StringToFloatImpl(T *val, const char *const str) {
  FLATBUFFERS_ASSERT(str && val);
  auto end = str;
  strtoval_impl(val, str, const_cast<char **>(&end));
  auto done = (end != str) && (*end == '\0');
  if (!done) *val = 0;
  return done;
}
```

This always sets `*val`.

OK so all the `StringToNumber` variants always initialize `val`. Let me look at the actual issue more carefully. The issue is "Use-of-uninitialized-value" in the scalar_fuzzer. Let me look at the `atot` function and how it's used in `ParseSingleValue`:

```cpp
if (check_now && IsScalar(match_type) && e.constant != "null") {
    switch (match_type) {
    #define FLATBUFFERS_TD(ENUM, IDLTYPE, CTYPE, ...) \
      case BASE_TYPE_ ## ENUM: {\
          CTYPE val; \
          ECHECK(atot(e.constant.c_str(), *this, &val)); \
          SingleValueRepack(e, val); \
        break; }
    FLATBUFFERS_GEN_TYPES_SCALAR(FLATBUFFERS_TD)
    #undef FLATBUFFERS_TD
    default: break;
    }
}
```

Here `CTYPE val;` is declared uninitialized, and `atot` is called. If `atot` fails (returns error), `val` is uninitialized. But `atot` always sets `*val` through `atot_scalar` -> `StringToNumber`. Wait, let me check `atot` again:

```cpp
template<typename T> CheckedError atot(const char *s, Parser &parser, T *val) {
  auto done = atot_scalar(s, val, bool_constant<is_floating_point<T>::value>());
  if (done) return NoError();
  if (0 == *val)
    return parser.Error("invalid number: \"" + std::string(s) + "\"");
  else
    return parser.Error("invalid number: \"" + std::string(s) + "\"" +
                        ", constant does not fit " + TypeToIntervalString<T>());
}
```

`atot_scalar` always sets `*val` through `StringToNumber`. So `val` is always initialized.

